WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:02.970
An order request still returns 201, but a

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.851
new build sends the order total as a
string.

00:00:06.022 --> 00:00:08.538
The API contract says it's an integer.

00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:10.857
Spec adherence helps you find that break.

00:00:11.419 --> 00:00:16.208
On the service page, open Contract and
attach the OpenAPI document.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:19.825
Then enable Spec Adherence in the replay's
test config.

00:00:19.996 --> 00:00:23.523
This screenshot shows the Contract section
on a demo service.

00:00:24.085 --> 00:00:28.673
In the mock-lab orders demo, the spec
requires total_cents to be an integer.

00:00:28.844 --> 00:00:32.287
The original build returns 3,600 as a
number.

00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:36.068
The changed build returns the same digits
inside quotes.

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:40.870
We checked both saved responses against the
same OpenAPI file.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.473
The original conforms.

00:00:42.644 --> 00:00:45.497
The changed response violates the schema at

00:00:45.497 --> 00:00:48.748
total_cents, even though the status is
still 201.

00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:52.927
In a dashboard report, open Readiness, then
Spec Adherence.

00:00:53.098 --> 00:00:58.097
Look under Fix in code for response type
mismatches, and use Requests to open

00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:01.343
Accuracy and inspect the calls behind the
finding.

00:01:01.885 --> 00:01:05.839
Correct the response type and replay again
against the same contract.
